# A bonus scene: meta files, small files the story puts beside the game, where the player
# can find them. main.vns includes this file and calls hana_file near the start of the
# story, so every new game writes the files again.
#
# The files go in the characters folder of the game folder, and nowhere else. Players
# can open, move, or delete them, and the story can check what they did. Every rule is in
# docs/script-language.md, "Meta files".

label hana_file:
    # "file write" puts a file in the characters folder. With only a name, it copies the
    # file of that name from the files folder: files/hana.chr, here a picture of Hana saved
    # under another name. The original in files/ never changes. A write can't be undone, so
    # the player can't roll back past it.
    file write "hana.chr"
    # With text after the name, file write writes that text into the file instead. \n
    # starts a new line in the file.
    file write "read_me.txt" "If you're reading this, don't tell Hana.\nShe can hear you."
    hana "Oh, and I left something for you, in your game folder. Look in the characters folder."
    hana happy "That's me in there. hana.chr. Don't open it, it's private."
    # "file delete" deletes a file the story wrote. Like a write, it can't be undone. The
    # original in files/ stays, so a new game writes the file again.
    file delete "hana.chr"
    hana surprised "...Huh. Did I just disappear from your folder?"
    hana "There's a copy of me in the files folder. You could put me back, if you like."
    thought "I wasn't sure what she meant. I let her go on anyway."
    # "file exists" sets a story variable to True when the file is in the characters
    # folder, and to False when it isn't. hana_here has a default in main.vns.
    file exists "hana.chr" into hana_here
    if hana_here:
        hana happy "You put me back! I knew you would."
    else:
        hana "Never mind. A new game always brings me back."
    return
